there were fewer rail workers than usual  at paris's gal de leon train station  because many were on strike to demand  higher wages staff say that they've not  had a pay rise in nearly a decade and  with a rising cost of living they're  struggling financially  we are on salaries and like many  employees we see the cost of living  going up but our salaries don't go up so  it doesn't add up  more than a third of france's train  services were cancelled causing  disruption for some travellers at the  start of the summer break  it is annoying for us as passengers but  the right to strike is a fundamental one  and i sympathize with their demands  with the holidays the strikes a bit  complicated also just after covert but  everyone has to defend their rights the  french government says that it cannot  raise people's wages but it is planning  a number of measures to help people with  inflation as part of a new cost of  living bill  the proposed law includes financial help  for poorer households with fuel and food  president emmanuel macron's government  no longer has a parliamentary majority  and has urged the opposition not to  block the bill  we will propose to extend the cap on gas  and electricity prices to increase  income from work and to lower charges on  the self-employed by tripling the upper  limits of the purchasing power bonus and  increased pensions and social benefits  the rail strike is expected to last one  day but with french airport workers  planning their third strike in a month  later this week it is clear that some  people's travel plans may not stay on  track natasha butler al jazeera paris
 

